 Component Selection and Justification

The AgriGuard system was designed using a combination of sensing, processing, control, user-interface and safety components. The components were selected based on the requirements of the system, including low power consumption, sufficient processing capability, multiple sensor inputs, automated control of irrigation zones, user safety, reliability and ease of integration.

The main components used in the system are:

| Component                        | Quantity | Primary Function                               |
| -------------------------------- | -------: | ---------------------------------------------- |
| ESP32                            |        1 | Main processing and control unit               |
| 4-Channel Relay Module           |        1 | Controls high-power irrigation devices         |
| Solenoid Irrigation Valves       |        3 | Controls water flow to individual zones        |
| Capacitive Soil-Moisture Sensors |        3 | Measures soil moisture in each irrigation zone |
| ON/OFF Switch                    |        1 | Main system power/control switch               |
| Emergency-Stop Switch            |        1 | Immediately stops normal system operation      |
| Reset Switch                     |        1 | Resets the system after an emergency stop      |
| Water-Level Sensor               |        1 | Monitors available water in the storage tank   |
| LCD Module                       |        1 | Displays system information to the user        |
| Pull-up Resistors                |        4 | Provides stable digital input states           |

---

## 1. ESP32 — Main Processing and Control Unit

The **ESP32** was selected as the main microcontroller unit (MCU) of AgriGuard.

The ESP32 is responsible for collecting sensor data, processing the information, executing the irrigation decision logic and controlling the irrigation hardware.

### Why the ESP32 was selected

The ESP32 is suitable for AgriGuard because it provides:

* Multiple digital and analogue GPIO pins
* Built-in Wi-Fi connectivity
* Sufficient processing power for real-time sensor processing
* Low-cost hardware
* Support for C/C++ programming
* Compatibility with common sensors and relay modules
* Sufficient memory for the irrigation-control software
* Ability to communicate with external APIs

The built-in Wi-Fi is particularly useful because AgriGuard can obtain environmental information from an online weather service. This allows the system to consider weather conditions alongside locally measured soil conditions.

The ESP32 also provides enough I/O capability to accommodate the three soil-moisture sensors, water-level sensing, switches, LCD communication and relay control.

### Role in AgriGuard

The ESP32 acts as the central controller:

```text
Soil Sensors ───────┐
                    │
Water-Level Sensor ─┤
                    │
Switches ───────────┤
                    ▼
                 ESP32
                    │
          ┌─────────┼─────────┐
          ▼         ▼         ▼
       Relays      LCD     Wi-Fi
          │                   │
          ▼                   ▼
      Valves              Weather Data
```

The ESP32 was therefore chosen instead of using a separate MPU, PLC or radio controller because the processing, control, connectivity and I/O requirements of the prototype can be handled by a single MCU.

